# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the stress harness.
#
# hml_get_args() allocates the argv-backed args array once at process
# startup and intentionally keeps it for the process lifetime (script
# args are globally reachable). It is a single fixed allocation, not
# unbounded growth — legitimately "still reachable at exit", not a
# leak. Suppress so `run_stress.sh lsan` measures only per-iteration
# leaks (the bug class this guard exists for).
leak:hml_get_args

# hml_socket_accept(): accepted client sockets are never freed —
# HmlSocket carries no refcount and HML_VAL_SOCKET is absent from
# hml_release (sockets are treated as "OS resource, shared by
# reference"). This is a real but small (~50 B + strdup'd peer addr
# per accepted connection) KNOWN gap, tracked for a socket-lifetime
# refcount change. Suppressed so the lsan lane stays green and keeps
# guarding the refcount/ownership leak class it was built for; remove
# this line when the socket-lifetime fix lands so the guard re-arms.
leak:hml_socket_accept
